docker-compose up -d --build
docker compose up -d --build portfolio-frontend portfolio-backend
# If you encounter issues with Docker build cache, you can clear it using the following commands:
Clear the Docker builder cache:
docker builder prune --all
Optionally, clear unused images and containers:
docker system prune --all
Re-run your Docker build command, possibly with the --no-cache flag:
docker-compose build --no-cache

docker compose up -d --build portfolio-frontend


To backup a MongoDb database, you can use the following script. Make sure to replace the placeholders with your actual MongoDB connection details and desired backup location.

chmod +x backup-mongo.sh
./backup-mongo.sh


Market-data service (Flask, port 5000) — Swagger UI for calling endpoints manually:

http://localhost:5000/apidocs        Swagger UI ("Try it out" on every endpoint)
http://localhost:5000/apispec.json   Raw OpenAPI spec

Endpoints:
POST /update/yahoo               Update market data via Yahoo Finance (body {"ticker": "AAPL"} = single; empty body = debounced batch over tickers queue)
POST /update/auto                Same, auto-routed by asset type (CRYPTO holding -> CoinGecko, else Yahoo)
POST /update/massive             Same, Massive API provider
POST /update/crypto              Same, CoinGecko provider (crypto tickers, e.g. BTC or BTC-USD; no API key)
POST /update/full                Full update for one ticker: market data + daily + monthly price history (body {"ticker": "AAPL"} required)
POST /update/sharesOutstanding   Backfill sharesOutstanding (body {"ticker": "AAPL"} = single; empty body = all marketData docs)
POST /update/exchangeRates       Fetch EUR FX rates from ECB
POST /history/refresh/<ticker>   Refresh daily price history for a ticker

Backend Swagger UI (Spring Boot): http://localhost:8080/swagger-ui.html


AI access (read-only MCP server)

Gives an AI chat client read-only access to portfolios, positions, performance,
dividends and ticker research.

Runs as a compose service in HTTP mode:

docker compose up -d --build portfolio-mcp        # -> http://localhost:8081/mcp

Set PORTFOLIO_USERNAME and PORTFOLIO_PASSWORD in .env first (same login as
http://localhost:3001). Client config is then just:

{ "mcpServers": { "portfolio": { "type": "http", "url": "http://localhost:8081/mcp" } } }

The endpoint has NO authentication of its own, so compose publishes it on 127.0.0.1
only. Do not change that to 0.0.0.0 without putting auth in front of it — anyone who
reaches the port gets read access to every portfolio the configured account owns.

The same image also runs in stdio mode (default Spring profile) when a client spawns
it with "docker run -i"; use that if you would rather nothing listened at all.

For stdio, mcp.json in this directory is the source of truth; register-mcp.mjs
writes it into Claude Code and/or Codex (they store MCP config differently):

node register-mcp.mjs --list        Show what is registered today
node register-mcp.mjs --dry-run     Show the changes without writing
node register-mcp.mjs               Register with both clients (user scope)
node register-mcp.mjs --scope local Register for this project only

It backs up every file it edits and keeps unrelated entries. Use ${PORTFOLIO_USERNAME}
and ${PORTFOLIO_PASSWORD} in mcp.json and export them in your shell rather than writing
credentials into the file; note Codex cannot expand variables, so the script resolves
them and the password ends up in plain text in ~/.codex/config.toml.

Full setup, the tool list and troubleshooting: portfolio-mcp-server/README.md

It authenticates with the same username and password used at http://localhost:3001,
supplied through the MCP client's env block. It cannot modify anything: the HTTP client
exposes only GET, every backend endpoint it calls is a GET, and each tool declares
readOnlyHint.

Backing REST layer (session auth required, same as the rest of /api/v1):
GET /api/v1/ai/portfolios                       Portfolios + currencies in use
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/snapshot           Positions, cash, totals, realized P&L
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/positions          Holdings only
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/diversification    By country / sector / industry / ticker
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/tags               Holdings grouped by user tag
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/transactions       Filtered, paged history
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/performance        Invested, value, P&L, XIRR, series
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/history            Month-end value per currency
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/realized-pnl       FIFO realized P&L per currency
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/dividends          Received, monthly/quarterly/yearly
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/dividend-calendar  Expected income by month
GET /api/v1/ai/{portfolioId}/watchlist          Watchlist + yield percentile ranking
GET /api/v1/ai/ticker/{ticker}                  Market data + key statistics
GET /api/v1/ai/ticker/{ticker}/historical       Dividends, splits, share counts, growth
GET /api/v1/ai/ticker/{ticker}/fundamentals     SEC EDGAR financials
GET /api/v1/ai/fx-rates                         Currency -> units per 1 EUR
GET /api/v1/ai/conventions                      How to read every response

Every response is {data, fxRates, baseCurrency, conversionFormula, notes}. Amounts stay
in each asset's native currency and are never converted, matching the rest of the API;
fxRates carries synthetic GBp/GBx entries (GBP x 100) so pence quotes convert with the
same formula as everything else.